/images/icon_out.png","alt":"External Web Site Icon","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/m5906a3f.gif","alt":"The figure shows the number of confirmed cases of 2009 pandemic influenza A (H1N1) virus infection, by rank and date patient reported to the ship's infirmary, during an outbreak on a Peruvian Navy ship during June-July 2009. Six weeks after departure, on June 25, 2009, one crew member reported to the infirmary with a 2-day history of fever of 101.3°F (38.5°C), sore throat, nasal congestion, headache, malaise, and cough. After undergoing a negative rapid influenza test, the patient was discharged from the infirmary with symptomatic treatment but was not placed in isolation. Two days later, on June 27, another crew member reported to the infirmary with similar symptoms that had begun 1 day before, including a temperature of 102.9°F (39.4°C); however, he tested positive for influenza A with the rapid test. This second patient shared living quarters with the first patient. The first patient was then retested with a rapid test and was found to be positive for influenza A.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":634,"renderedHeight":304,"naturalWidth":634,"naturalHeight":304,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 981 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":26963},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_pdf.gif","alt":"Adobe PDF file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"de